EXT. FOXHOLE LINE - ARDENNES FOREST - DAWN (1944)

Snow. Frozen trees. Breath hanging in the air. A row of hastily dug holes, sandbag-lipped, branches over the top. Smoke rises from a tin can fire hidden in a pit.

SGT. MORGENSTERN, 40s, a rangy Wisconsin man with an icicle in his mustache, crouches over the fire, warming his hands. CPL. WU, 20s, scratching at a frostbitten ear, sits on an ammo box. LT. FAIRCHILD, 20s, fresh-faced, in a clean helmet, studies a map with a pencil. PVT. BEAUMONT, 19, hugging his rifle, shifts from foot to foot. PVT. TSOSIE, 22, a Navajo man, crouches at the tree line watching the fog. DOC KOWALSKI, 30s, rummages in a bag, producing a tin of cold coffee.

FAIRCHILD
Sergeant, how much longer?

MORGENSTERN
Till what, sir?

FAIRCHILD
Till we move.

MORGENSTERN
Till they tell us.

FAIRCHILD
And when's that?

MORGENSTERN
Hell if I know.

WU
(to himself)
Everybody asks the same question.

BEAUMONT
Is it quiet because they're gone?

TSOSIE
(without turning)
No.

BEAUMONT
How do you know?

TSOSIE
The birds. There aren't any.

KOWALSKI
Coffee, anyone?

He hands out cups. Each man takes one in silence. Wu sips and winces.

WU
This is awful.

KOWALSKI
It's hot.

WU
Hot isn't a flavor, Doc.

MORGENSTERN
(to the group)
Eat. Drink. Rest. Because soon we won't.

FAIRCHILD
Sergeant.

MORGENSTERN
Lieutenant.

FAIRCHILD
Are we going to be all right?

MORGENSTERN
(a long pause, then)
Sir, I've been asked that nine hundred times, and I've never once lied.

FAIRCHILD
That's not an answer.

MORGENSTERN
It's the best one I've got.

Beaumont drains his cup, stares at the bottom of it. Tsosie, still at the tree line, raises two fingers without looking back.

TSOSIE
Quiet. Something's moving.

They all freeze. Morgenstern is on his feet before the word finishes. Fairchild crouches. Wu snatches his rifle. Kowalski drops the coffee tin.

A deer steps out of the fog. It looks at them with huge, calm, dark eyes. Then it turns and walks, unhurried, back into the trees.

No one speaks. Then Beaumont laughs, a single sharp breath, and Wu joins, and one by one they lower their weapons, until even Tsosie smiles faintly at the snow.

MORGENSTERN
(muttering)
Damn, that's a good-looking buck.

WU
Don't even think about it, Sarge.

MORGENSTERN
I'm thinking about venison stew.

The fire crackles. Snow begins to fall.
